are less useful for their own combinations.\n\nLooking at the B7: in their hand, they have one B7. If they discard it, maybe someone could use it for a chow. The B7 count is 3 left. Not too high. But if they need B7 for their own hand, maybe not. However, since they have B6 and B9, maybe B7 is part of a possible B6-7-8 chow. But since they don't have B8, maybe it's not useful. Alternatively, B7 could be part of a sequence with B6 and B7, but without B8, not helpful. So maybe B7 is not needed for their hand.\n\nT8 has two in their hand. If they discard one, someone might Chi if they have T7 or T9. But the T8 count is 4 left, so not too bad. W5: they have one. The W5 count is 4 left. If they discard W5, someone could use it for a chow. But since their own hand has W2-7, maybe keeping W5 is part of a middle chow (like W4-5-6). They have W4, W5, W6. So that's a possible chow. So discarding W5 might break that possibility. So maybe not a good idea.\n\nW2: they have one. The W2 count is 3 left. If they discard W2, someone could Chi if they have W3 or W1. But since the next player already has a Chi of W2-3-4, maybe they are already using that suit. Discarding W2 might be safe if they don't need it for their own chows. But since they have W2, W3, W4, maybe keeping W2 is part of that chow. So discarding W2 could be bad if they need it for a chow.\n\nB6: they have one. The B6 count is 3 left. If they discard B6, someone could use it for a chow with B5 or B7. They have B7 and B9, but no B8. So B6 could be part of a B5-6-7 chow if they have B5. But they don't have B5. So maybe not needed. Discarding B6 might be safe.\n\nW7: they have one. The W7 count is 4 left. If they discard W7, someone could use it for a chow with W6 or W8. They have W6 and W7, so maybe part of a W6-7-8 chow if they get a W8. But they don't have W8. So not sure. Discarding W7 might be okay.\n\nT9: they have one. The T9 count is 3 left. If they discard T9, someone could Chi if they have T7-8-9. They have T8 and T9, so maybe part of a T7-8-9 chow. But they don't have T7. So discarding T9 might be safe.\n\nB9: they have two. The B9 count is 3 left. Discarding one B9 might be safe, especially since they have two. If they discard B9, someone could use it for a chow with B7-8-9 if they have B8. But they don't have B8. So maybe not needed.\n\nW4: they have one. The W4 count is 3 left. If they discard W4, someone could Chi if they have W3 or W5. Since they have W3 and W4 and W5, that's part of a chow. So discarding W4 might break that. Not ideal.\n\nW3: they have two. The W3 count is 3 left. If they discard a W3, they still have one left. Since they have two, maybe discarding one is okay. But since W3 is part of the W2-3-4 chow and possibly others, maybe keeping it is better.\n\nSo considering all this, the B7 and B6 might be the least useful for their own hand. Between B7 and B6, maybe B7 is better to discard because B6 could be part of a possible chow if they get a B5 and B7. But since they don't have B5 or B8, maybe not. Alternatively, if they discard B7, it's less likely to be needed for their own hand. Also, looking at the valid actions, B7 is an option. Let me check the counts again: B7 has 3 left. Not too bad. So maybe playing B7 is a good move here.\n\nAnother option is T8. They have two, so discarding one might be safe. But T8 is part of a possible T7-8-9 chow if they get T7. However, since they don't have T7, maybe not. But if they discard T8, someone might Chi if they have T7 or T9. But they have T9, so if someone has T7 and T8, they could form a chow. But since the user has T9, maybe T8 is part of a T8-9 pair. But they have two T8s and one T9. So maybe keeping T8 is better for a pair or part of a chow if they get a T7.\n\nBetween B7 and T8, I think B7 is the safer discard because it's less likely to be needed for the user's own hand. So the answer would be to play B7.\n", "output": "Evaluation: The player has a concealed hand with multiple potential chows in Characters (W2-W7) and possible pairs in T8/B9. B7 is a less critical tile for forming key melds and has a moderate remaining count (3), making it a safe discard.\n\nReason: Discarding B7 avoids disrupting potential chows in Characters (W2-W4 and W5-W7) and T8/T9 sequences. Keeping W3s and W4-W7 maintains flexibility for multiple chows. B7 has limited utility for the player's hand and is less likely to trigger opponents' melds compared to other tiles.\n\nAnswer: Play B7"}
