ecause it's not part of any potential chow. Playing T8 is better because it's part of a pair. \n\nTherefore, the best action is to play B7.\n", "output": "Evaluation: The hand has potential for two chows in Characters (W2-3-4 and W5-6-7). Discarding B7 preserves key chow tiles and avoids breaking sequential melds. Keeping T8 (two copies) supports a possible pair, while B7 is not part of any current or potential chow.\n\nReason: Playing B7 removes a non-critical tile not involved in sequential melds, preserving the W2-3-4 and W5-6-7 chows. This maintains flexibility for forming two chows and a pair, which aligns with scoring patterns like Pure Straight (24 points) or Half Flush (6 points). Discarding a terminal tile (T9) or a middle tile (W5) would risk breaking sequences.\n\nAnswer: Play B7"}
